=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — the default gateway 192.168.200.1 "dropping out for a few
seconds at random" — does not exist in the live lab. Exhaustive inspection of
every component in the Location C forwarding path found no anomaly.

Router4's LAN interface eth0 (192.168.200.1/24) is UP/LOWER_UP with zero RX
errors, zero TX drops, zero carrier-state changes, and zero collisions since
lab startup. There is no tc netem loss or delay rule on eth0, switch4, switch5,
or switch6. Spanning Tree is disabled on all three switches, so no port can
enter a blocking state. No keepalived, VRRP, Corosync, or any other first-hop
redundancy daemon is running on any device in the path; there is no virtual IP
that could fail over and cause a brief outage.

The RIP domain is stable: router4 announces 192.168.200.0/24 as a directly
connected network, both of its RIP neighbours (router3 via 172.25.1.0/24 and
router5 via 192.168.15.0/24) report zero bad-packets and zero bad-routes, and
no other router in the topology injects a competing advertisement for that
prefix. The FRR watchfrr, zebra, and ripd daemons are all running cleanly; the
startup file contains no injected kill scripts or interface-flap commands.

Live reachability confirms the finding: 50 ICMP probes spread across all four
Location C endpoints (PC11–PC14) targeting 192.168.200.1 returned 0% packet
loss at sub-2 ms latency.

Root cause: There is no network fault. The helpdesk ticket is a false premise;
the gateway is stable and fully operational.

Corrective fix: No network change is required. Advise the users to recheck
their local host-side configuration (NIC driver, ARP cache, or application
timeout settings) and close the ticket as a false alarm.
=== END ===
